const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "et:2": [
    "In the highlands of Kaffa, in Ethiopia, a shepherd called Kaldi watched his goats among green bushes. One day he saw them leaping and almost dancing, as if the grass had music. He found it strange, because the goats were usually calm at that hour. He looked at the ground and saw shiny red berries, some already bitten. The goats had eaten from that bush. Curious, Kaldi picked up a handful and kept looking at the bright colour in his palm. He did not yet know what the world would drink.",
    "He tasted one berry, then another, and felt sleep walk away. It was as if the sun had entered his chest. His legs wanted a path. His heart beat happily, not sickly. Kaldi laughed at the goats, which were still jumping, and filled a cloth bag. He thought of the monastery in the valley, where the monks prayed for long hours. Sometimes they nodded off from tiredness. If the plant woke the goats, perhaps it would wake the night prayer too. He went down the slope with the bag on his shoulder and a light step.",
    "When the monks saw the berries, they were afraid. They said it could be bad fruit, a trick, and ordered it thrown on the fire. The berries fell among the embers and, instead of a sour smell, a sweet strong scent filled the room. A younger monk came closer and took out some roasted beans. He mixed them with hot water. They drank carefully. That night the prayer did not fall. Their eyes stayed open, and their steady voices held on until morning.",
    "Since then the people of Kaffa have learned to roast, to grind and to share. The coffee ceremony, which they call buna, is a ritual of hospitality, not a rush to swallow. There is incense, and there is roasting in front of whoever has arrived. Then comes a turn of the small cup, in friendly silence. A child sits nearby and breathes in the smoke and the bean. Nobody drinks alone if there is a neighbour at the door. The plant left the plateau, but the gesture of offering stayed at home, slowly.",
    "It may be a legend, and it is honest to say so. The plant, though, is Ethiopian, and the whole world drinks this goat leap without knowing Kaldi's name. From Kaffa, coffee went to other ports, other countries, other tables. The origin the people chose is still the shepherd, the dancing goats and the monks who were afraid first. If you drink slowly and offer someone a cup, you have already understood the tale. The fruit was not bad. It was an invitation to stay awake with others.",
  ],
  "et:3": [
    "In the highlands of Ethiopia, night comes down cold, and the wind runs without asking permission. A young shepherd called Dawit guarded the flock in an open place. The teff had already been harvested there. The geladas, monkeys with long capes of hair, could be seen far off on the rocks. They sat still as old men. Dawit knew fear, because the wolf of these hills is a real hunger, not a drawing from a book. He counted the sheep one by one and marked each with a coloured thread. That way none got lost in the dark without a name.",
    "His younger brother, Tesfaye, brought bread and a small drum. The family rule was clear: you do not sleep on the open side. You do not stay alone when the moon is thin. They sat with their backs to a stone, with the fire low and the flock inside a circle of branches. Far off came a step, then another. The wolf's eyes shone between two bushes. Dawit did not shout. He put his hand on his brother's arm and remembered their grandmother's words. If the wolf hears two voices, it hesitates and does not rush in.",
    "Tesfaye beat the drum, not as at a party, but like someone calling a whole village. The sound went down the valley and came back off the stones, thick, like many feet. Dawit spoke loudly, counting the sheep by their threads. He wanted the wolf to understand the flock had a keeper who was awake. The animal circled, sniffed the wind and did not step into the light. Hunger pushed it, but the drum and the two voices held it at the edge. Nobody threw a stone at it. It was enough not to be alone on the slope.",
    "The night was long. One sheep took fright and ran to the open side. Dawit went to fetch it without letting go of his stick. He talked softly so that it would not push into the scrub. Tesfaye did not stop the drum. When the sheep came back to the circle, the blue thread was still round its neck. The count closed again. The wolf stayed a while longer, waiting for a mistake, and at last turned towards the rocks. The two brothers did not sleep at the same time. One kept watch while the other rested his face in his hands.",
    "In the morning the wolf's track was in the cold earth, and the flock was whole. Rural Ethiopia tells this without decoration, because a child who tends animals already knows the fear of night. The legend does not promise that the wolf will vanish from the world. It gives a trick and a rule: count the sheep, do not sleep on the open side, call your brother. A wolf that hears two voices hesitates. The teff, the fire and the geladas on the rocks are part of the same warning. Courage, here, is company, and a drum beats a shout alone.",
  ],
  "et:4": [
    "In Axum, in northern Ethiopia, the faith of the people says something huge in a quiet voice. The Ark of the Covenant is the holy box of the story of Menelik. It is kept in a church on the hill. A monk looks after it and does not leave that place, year after year. Nobody from outside sees it. There is no visit and no door open to the curious. The whole country, in a way, guards that box. The monk's silence is part of the story, as important as the old stone of the church of St Mary.",
    "It is not proved with the eyes, and the elders do not get cross at that sentence. They say: it is lived. Every Ethiopian church has a copy, the tabot, in its most private place. They call that place the holy of holies. The tabot is not a toy or a decoration. It is the sign that the great box in Axum has sisters of cloth and wood all over the country. So even those who never climb the hill take part in the guard. The Ark does not fit in one hand only. It fits in a whole community.",
    "At the festival of Timkat, a child sees the tabot covered and carried in procession. People leap and sing around it. Nobody lifts the cloth to peep. The respect is exactly not seeing. The blessed water falls, the drums answer, and the streets fill with white. The Ark, visible or not in Axum, passes through the street in the form of cloth. A girl asks her grandmother whether the real box looks like this. The grandmother smiles and says: ‘You guard what you love even when you show it to nobody.’",
    "The monk of Axum, in legend and in faith, eats little and speaks little. He would not trade the place for any curiosity in the world. Some people travel from far away just to be near the wall. They do not ask for a show. The hill stays quiet. Inside, it is said, the box remains. Outside, life goes on, among market, prayer and children running. The two things do not push each other aside. Ethiopia has learned to hold a sacred secret without turning it into a display. The mystery, here, is everyday work.",
    "The child grows and understands something. The country does not need to open the box for the story to be true in the heart. The hill, the monk and the silence make the best-kept myth in Africa, and it is told with reverence. If you enter an Ethiopian church, you may not see the tabot. That too is an answer. The Ark of the legend teaches how to keep without grabbing. Some things are honoured with lowered eyes, in procession, with the cloth on top. The people sing, and the hill keeps watch over Axum.",
  ],
  "tz:0": [
    "In the Chagga myth of Tanzania, Kilimanjaro did not come about all at once. The two peaks, Kibo and Mawenzi, were brothers. Each had a campfire high up, so the night on the mountain would not be left without a flame. Kibo was calm and looked after the embers as one looks after a younger brother. Mawenzi was forgetful, liked to come down to play and forgot to return in time. For the Chagga, the mountain is Ruwa's home, and the snow to come will be something sacred, not decoration.",
    "One night Mawenzi let his fire go out. The wind took the last spark, and his peak went dark and cold. He came down to Kibo, ashamed, and asked to borrow some embers. Kibo was stirring a pot with a big wooden spoon, because his fire still burned slowly and well. He did not refuse the help, but he was angry at the carelessness. The legend says he struck him with the stirring spoon, a blow from an angry brother, not a war. The whole mountain heard the crack.",
    "Mawenzi was marked. The peak, which had been smooth, became full of holes and cuts. Teeth of stone jutted out, like a face the spoon had scratched. Kibo never let his fire die, so he stayed upright and soft. His outline was calm, like someone who does the job. There was no blood to tell of. There was shape. Whoever looks at the two summits today sees the quarrel still written in the rock. One brother is torn by carelessness, the other whole because he kept the fire until morning.",
    "In the end Kibo received the snow. It was the white ash of a well-tended fire. It was a pale hat that never melts in the story, even when the sun is strong. Mawenzi stayed dark and rough, without that coat. The Chagga say the snow is sacred. It is the prize of those who do not let the flame die. All of Tanzania sees the peak from far off, from savannah and town. People know the brothers without needing to climb. Geology speaks of ancient eruptions. The legend speaks of a spoon. Both explain the same outline.",
    "The Chagga child hears the names and learns the message without any shouting. Whoever lets the fire die ends up full of holes, open to the wind that passes through. Whoever tends it receives the white hat. It is not an invitation to hit anyone. It is a warning: look after what you have been given. That may be a flame, a house, or a brother who asks for embers. If you see Kilimanjaro at the edge of the sky, remember Kibo and Mawenzi. The great mountain is still telling their quarrel.",
  ],
  "tz:1": [
    "In the Swahili language, the clever hare is called Sungura, and her tales travel along the whole coast of Tanzania. Sungura lends, lies, runs away and, sometimes, teaches. One day she boasted in front of the elephant. ‘I can pull harder than you,’ she said. The elephant laughed through his trunk. He accepted a tug-of-war, set for the next morning by the river. Sungura left that spot very upright, as if she had already won. In her chest, though, she knew a small body does not beat a giant by brute strength.",
    "On the other side of the water lived the hippo, as strong as the elephant and as easy to annoy. Sungura repeated the sentence, changing only the name, and the hippo agreed too. The hare asked for a long rope and gave each of them the same order. ‘You pull when I shout, and stop only when I say enough.’ Neither of them saw the other. The wide brown river was the curtain. Sungura, in the middle, tied both ends to the same rope, hidden in the reeds. She held the knot between her paws, very pleased.",
    "In the morning she shouted from both sides. The elephant pulled. The hippo pulled. The rope groaned, the water jumped, and the giants were fighting each other without knowing it. Sungura, in the middle, put on a theatre of effort. She groaned, slipped, and begged the rope to take pity. Anyone passing along the bank thought the hare was a marvel. She was not even really sweating. She was sweating with held-back laughter. For a good while the trick went well. The savannah seemed to side with her just because she spoke louder.",
    "The elephant took one more step. The hippo did the same. In the end they saw each other in the middle of the river, with the rope still in their hands. There was no hare holding the weight. Sungura was on a trunk, the theatre over. The giants did not crush her. They were tired, wet and angry in a way that was also laughter. ‘You did not measure strength,’ said the elephant. ‘You measured our trust.’ The hare had to come down, say sorry and help dry the rope. It was small work for someone who thought herself enormous.",
    "Swahili storytelling took this tale along the whole coast, from campfire to campfire. Tanzania laughs, and a child who copies Sungura gets a loving telling-off before pudding. Cunning without kindness is also told, so the price can be seen. The next day nobody wanted to lend her even a grain. Sungura is not always good. She is always memorable. If you win a game with a hidden knot, the victory lasts a short time. The true rope is the one others agree to hold with you again.",
  ],
  "tz:2": [
    "Where Lake Victoria is today, the legend says there was dry land. The lake is also called Nyanza and Ukerewe. It had villages with pots by the doors. The lake is so big that the shore looks like a sea. Its origin had to be big too. The Baganda, the Sukuma and the Luo tell different versions. In some places a hole wept until it filled. In others a god poured out a bottomless pot. The version the children of the shore repeat begins with a thirsty old man and a closed door.",
    "The old man arrived at the end of the afternoon, with dust on his feet and a hoarse voice. He asked for just one sip, not a banquet. The village had water in the well, but it was afraid to share. ‘If we give to one, tomorrow ten will come,’ said someone, and the door stayed half closed. The old man did not shout. He thanked them for the nothing they gave him and went on along the dry path, more slowly. A child wanted to run after him with a cup. The mother held her hand. That cup which never left is the quiet centre of the story.",
    "At night the earth groaned. Some say the well hole began to weep. Some say the pot in the sky turned over whole. The water rose without cruel haste, but without stopping, and the low houses had to be left. The people went up to the high ground, with children in their arms and empty pots knocking against their legs. Nobody was left under the water just so the legend could turn into a fright. The village lost the ground because it had refused a sip. Now the ground has become the horizon.",
    "In the morning there was no path, there was a lake. The old man was not on the shore pointing a finger. It was the water that had stayed, enormous, remembering his thirst. The boats, later, learned to cross what had been a street. The fishermen gave names to the winds. The children grew up seeing only water to the edge of their eyes. They believed any version, because all of them say the same thing. This ground was opened to give us water to drink. The size of the lake is the size of the warning.",
    "Tanzania shares this lake with Kenya and Uganda, and so the legend has no border drawn on the wave. The old man who was refused water crosses all three countries. He does it whenever someone closes the door on a traveller. The stranger's thirst, in this story, makes or unmakes an inland sea. If you have a pot and see thirst at the door, the shore asks you for that sip first. The rest of the water, the enormous lake, was already the lesson. It belongs to those who came too late for the cup.",
  ],
  "tz:3": [
    "On the savannah of Tanzania, the baobab is no ordinary log. It is a square with a trunk, and inside it, says the legend, lives the spirit of the ancestor. The tree was already old when your grandfather's grandfather was born. It will still be there when today's children have grandchildren. The hollow holds voices, bats and, in the dry season, a scrap of shade. That shade is worth more than gold. The meetings are held around it. Nobody marks the place with a flag. The tree is the place, and it was there before the conversation.",
    "Some swear the baobab walks at night, slowly, shifting a hand's width. It moves to be nearer to those who need shade. The children listen and look in the morning to see if the roots have taken a step. There is no fear of being chased. There is respect for an old age that does not fit in a clock. If the wind moves the dry branches, the village says the spirit is greeting. Whoever answers softly, in the voice of a visitor, does well. Whoever shouts just to prove courage hears the echo and realises the grace was small.",
    "A traveller, in a hurry for fire, put a flame to the trunk to get easy wood. The flame did not even manage to grow. The smoke blew back in his face. The echo inside the hollow said the name of the village. The man put it all out with his feet, his heart tight. The spirit did not chase him through the grass. It charged him another way. The traveller understood he was burning a century, not a branch. He sat down far off, said sorry out loud, and waited for the trunk to cool.",
    "Whoever rests in the shade and leaves a little millet beer by the root goes on their way without weight. It is not a payment to fear. It is good manners, like taking off your hat in a house that is not yours. A girl went into the hollow trunk. She heard her own voice come back deeper and called it her grandfather's voice. The legend confirms it: it is a voice. She left the millet and came out without breaking a branch. She told her mother. Her mother did not laugh. The echo is family too, she said.",
    "Inland Tanzania treats the baobab as a square, a market of shade and a living archive. It is not cut as a fallen log is cut. A century is cut, and centuries get angry. Their anger may be only silence and a lack of shelter. The bats stay on the hollow ceiling, the rain water is kept in the folds, and the tree remains. If you sit in its shade, speak softly and leave something small. The spirit does not ask for wealth. It asks you to recognise a life older than your hurry.",
  ],
  "tz:4": [
    "Zanzibar, in the Indian Ocean, smells of cloves and closes the night with carved wooden doors. In Swahili stories, an enormous turtle crosses the channel between the island and the mainland. It carries messages that people, too busy, do not want to carry slowly. Sometimes it is said to be an enchanted sultan. Sometimes it is the grandmother of the sea, kin to the giant turtles that lived in these islands. The mixture is the city itself. Arab, African and Indian tales sit on the same mat, not arguing who came first.",
    "A child from Stone Town lost a message from her grandmother for the family on the other shore. The boats were full and nobody wanted to turn back for a small sentence. The turtle lifted its head in the shallow water by the quay. The child, without fear, asked for help. ‘I am slow,’ said the turtle. ‘If the sentence is true, I will arrive on time for what matters.’ The child repeated the message. Her grandmother was well. The door of the house was still open. There was dried clove waiting for whoever came.",
    "The turtle entered the channel while the sun was still high. The dolphins passed it, laughing at their own hurry. A monsoon wind tried to push it off its way. It tucked its head in a little, without letting go of the sentence. On the other side, the family watched the horizon and thought the news had failed. The turtle brought no flag and no drum. It brought only the child's voice, kept as one keeps a seed under the tongue. That seed was safe from salt and from forgetting.",
    "It arrived at the end of the light, wet and in no hurry for applause. It said the whole sentence, without removing one word. The family lit a small fire on the beach. That way the child, on the other side, would know the message had a harbour. Stone Town that night looked like an old book open on the sea. It smelled of sweet fruit and hot stone. The turtle asked for no kingdom. It asked that next time the sentence should leave earlier, so the tide would not have to do people's work. Then it went back into the water.",
    "The child of Zanzibar grows up hearing Swahili, Arabic and this turtle in the same evening. The legend is the island: slow, ancient, crossing waters that maps treat as a shortcut. Whoever looks at Zanzibar only as a beach loses the message and keeps the sand. The sand is also beautiful, but not enough. The turtle, stone on the doors or flesh in the channel, asks for time. The clove dries slowly, the carved wood takes months, and the sea is not crossed with shouting. The same pace serves all three.",
  ],
  "sn:0": [
    "On the savannah of Senegal, the Wolof campfire knows two names by heart. Leuk, the hare, is small, quick and full of ideas. Bouki, the hyena, is strong, noisy and always hungry. The two walk the same sandy paths, among acacias and a big sky. Leuk steals a little, lies a little and, in the end, sometimes teaches. Bouki never learns the lesson, and so she comes back the next day. The books of Birago Diop and Senghor kept this pair. A child who only wants to be clever has not yet heard the price.",
    "One afternoon Leuk saw a pot of honey in the shade of a tree. Bouki was coming too, nose in the air. The hare said: ‘This honey is the moon's. Only someone who fits in the pot can taste it without spoiling the magic.’ Greedy Bouki pushed her head right in. She got stuck. She grumbled and grumbled. Leuk ate a small spoonful and wiped her mouth. Then she went to call the village to free the hyena. Nobody laughed with cruelty. The children laughed all the same. Bouki was left with her fur stuck down and the same hunger.",
    "The next night, Leuk asked Bouki to help guard a sack of millet. She promised half. Then she moved the sack while the hyena slept with her mouth open. In the morning Bouki found only straw. The hare danced on the sand and called her scatterbrained. The savannah laughed with the hyena's teeth and the hare's eyes. This time nobody freed a pot, because the pot was trust, and trust is not washed off with water. Bouki sat down, hurt, not understanding the game.",
    "The village griot called Leuk to the fire and put the sack of millet in the middle. He said that cleverness without kindness is also told, so the price can be seen. Leuk was not only tricks. She lowered her ears and shared the millet for real. Half went to Bouki, and a handful to those who were hungry. The hyena ate slowly, still suspicious. The hare understood that teaching in the worst way leaves a bitter taste. It leaves it even when the audience claps. Paying for the trick was part of the old story.",
    "Wolof children still copy Leuk's leap, and the elders make them think about how Bouki feels. In this pair, Senegal has its own Anansi. A small one beats the strong, and the strong one reminds us not to humiliate the slow. The legend carries both messages at the same time. The savannah in the middle does not choose just one laugh. If you are clever, share the millet. If you are strong, listen before pushing your head into the pot.",
  ],
  "sn:1": [
    "Off Dakar lies the island of Gorée. It is small, with soft-coloured houses, an old tree, and the sea very close to the doors. The elders of Senegal do not tell it as a tale about mermaids. They tell it as memory. Portuguese and French ships passed through that port in hard centuries. Many people were taken by force to the other side of the Atlantic. The island today receives visits, flowers and silence. The story, even told in a low voice, remains the most serious in the country.",
    "Awa went with her grandmother to the House of Slaves, a plain building facing the water. Her grandmother held her hand and showed her a narrow opening, the Door of No Return. She explained, without shouting, that people left through that door. They had chosen neither the boat nor the destination. There were no storybook monsters on the other side, there was a cruel market in lives. Awa stayed quiet. What matters is not hidden from children. People left by force, and the names should not be lost.",
    "They say on the island that the spirits still look at the Atlantic and call the names. They call them one by one, like people who will not accept being forgotten. Awa saw no figures. She saw the sea as it always was, blue and wide, and flowers laid on the stone. Her grandmother murmured a name from the old family and asked her granddaughter to repeat it with respect. The wind carried the syllables out through the door. Nobody sweetened that place with a mermaid song. The sea in front was the same sea of farewell.",
    "In the narrow streets, a guide spoke of Gorée as one speaks of a shrine, not of a park. He asked for silence by the door and let each person stay as long as they needed. Awa put a small flower on the step and took no laughing photographs. She understood that visiting is not consuming a fright. It is remembering. The House of Slaves does not ask for make-believe. It asks that the truth fit in one clear sentence. That door was not a free exit, so the return has to be made in memory.",
    "When the boat went back to Dakar, Awa was still watching the island grow small. Her grandmother told her that telling Gorée is a duty. It is like lighting a lamp in a dark room without inventing too many ghosts. The names, if said carefully, come back a little. No mermaid can take the place of the Door of No Return. There is the sea, the flowers, the silence, and the promise not to let the story fall quiet. That is how Senegal teaches you to look the Atlantic in the face.",
  ],
  "sn:2": [
    "In Senegal, the baobab is not just a tree. It is the village square, the house of shade, and the place where the griot sits down to speak. The trunk can be so wide that several people lean on it at once. The hollow inside sends the voice back like an echo. The children call that echo a mouth. The elders smile and do not deny it quickly. Senghor called the baobab a column. The people, more at home, simply call it our roof of bark.",
    "An old tale says the baobab keeps, in its wood, the words of those who have gone. If the griot touches the trunk the right way, the tree gives back a proverb, not a shout. In Awa's village, the griot Iba knew that touch. One afternoon, two brothers were quarrelling over a broken jug and would not listen to their grandmother. Iba took them into the shade and put his palm on the rough bark. He asked for silence. The wind went into the hollow and came out as a short, old and clear sentence.",
    "The sentence said: ‘Broken water does not return to the jug, but a home returns if the hands say sorry.’ The brothers looked at each other. They were still angry. Then they looked at the ground. It was no flash of lightning magic. It was the griot's touch, the wind and a tree so old that it seems to listen. Awa, sitting on the root, swore that the two sounds were one. The grandmother's proverb, said years before under the same crown, had soaked into the wood. It waited for someone patient to let it loose.",
    "Iba explained that a baobab is not cut the way any log is cut. Cutting that trunk would be cutting a century. Centuries get angry when memory has no stage. The village gathers there to decide, to rest and to listen. The holes in the bark, the bats at dusk and the wide shade are part of the house. They are not part of a fright. Whoever leaves a little water by the root, or only a thank you, does well. Whoever treats the tree as firewood loses the village's microphone.",
    "Awa came back the next day and put her ear to the trunk, without demanding miracles. She heard the wind and a bird. Perhaps she also heard the sentence still living in the wood. Without the baobab, Senegal would lose a place where words are not lost. With it, every village has a stage made of bark. The legend joins the griot and the echo, and the two are enough. Words, in this story, do not fly far away. They stay in the tree, waiting for the next child who knows how to listen.",
  ],
  "sn:3": [
    "Anansi, the clever spider of Akan tales, was not born in Senegal. It is fair to say so before the story begins. On the coast and in the towns, though, tales cross borders like people. Leuk, the hare, already does Anansi's work around here: the small one who wins by ideas. One harmattan day, the Sahel air turned dry and white. A spider came down from a branch and introduced itself. It was visiting. Leuk offered it shade and a sip of water, because teranga, hospitality, also fits on a leaf.",
    "The two sat down like cousins and swapped tricks with no heavy cruelty. Anansi taught a web knot that holds a calabash without breaking it. Leuk taught a whistle that makes sand seem like rain. They laughed quietly, so as not to wake Bouki the hyena. She slept on an empty belly near a sack of millet. The spider said: ‘In my forest I work alone. Here, with you, the lesson can stretch wider.’ The hare wiggled her ears, curious. She accepted a partner for one day.",
    "When Bouki woke, she wanted the whole sack. She even demanded that the small ones carry her food. Leuk and Anansi did not hit her or frighten her with monsters. They made the calabash roll away by itself across the sand, held by an almost invisible thread. The hyena ran after it until she was dizzy and out of breath. At every turn the calabash looked closer and slipped away. The two clever neighbours were, in fact, too much for Bouki. The savannah saw the number of them and laughed. The story was not over with laughter.",
    "In the late afternoon, Bouki sat down, tired and empty-handed. She had learned nothing but anger. Leuk looked at Anansi and remembered the old campfire: cleverness also pays. They cut the thread, stopped the calabash and left the hyena an honest bowl of millet, plus some water. Bouki ate, still grumbling, and this time she was not left by the well or in the dark. Anansi said a trick with no plate at the end teaches little. The hare agreed, with her ears a little lower.",
    "The Senegalese child laughs at this meeting and sees that West African folklore is a family. Senegal, land of teranga, welcomes even spiders from other nations, as long as they come in peace. Leuk is still the trickster of the house, and Anansi is the visiting cousin. Poor Bouki did not ask for two such lively neighbours. But the story gives her them, so the lesson stretches wide. Strength alone tires. Cleverness, if it is hospitable, still knows how to share the sack.",
  ],
  "sn:4": [
    "Saint-Louis, in northern Senegal, is an island of bridges over the river that borders Mauritania. The fishermen and the washerwomen know a lady they call Maam Kumba Bang. She is not a market joke. She is the protector of the city, the lady of the river. She appears dressed in white when the water asks for respect. Some leave a flower, a little milk or a quiet song on the bank. Without her, they say, the island would be only sand and bridges. With her, it has an owner.",
    "Awa went with her aunt to take washing to the calm arm of the river. It was the end of the afternoon. In the light and the water she saw a woman in white. Her feet barely touched the surface, and she was looking at the bridges. Awa did not call anyone with shouting. Her aunt put a finger to her lips and murmured the name: Maam Kumba Bang. The lady did not ask for fear. She asked that the water not be treated as a rubbish bin. A boat had left litter on the bank that day.",
    "The fish had kept away, as if their house had shut its door. For two days the smell rose and the nets came back light. Some laughed and said it was only the wind. The aunt did not laugh. She explained that Maam Kumba guards Saint-Louis like an old patron saint, city and river at once. Whoever dirties the arm of water offends the whole city. Awa called two cousins, and together they picked up the litter. They washed the stones and left a white flower where the lady had stood. The apology was made with hands.",
    "At dusk the water cleared enough to show the sandy bottom. A fisherman came back with his net shining with small fish. On the bridge, someone swore they saw the white again moving away. There was no threat and no hurry. Awa did not need to prove the figure to sceptics. She needed to keep the rule: the river is not a crate. The offerings, the stories of those who saw her and the warning travel together in Saint-Louis. Northern Senegal does not joke with this name.",
    "Maam Kumba Bang still watches the tides and the bridges. The children listen to her as one listens to the lady of the house. The Senegal River, wide and bordering, has this calm guard. If you go to Saint-Louis, throw nothing into the water. Do not speak of the river as if nobody lived there. The legend is ancient and of the city at the same time. The island of sand stands on its bridges, yes. It also stands because someone in white still asks that it be treated as a lady.",
  ],
  "tn:0": [
    "In Tunisia, before Tunis was the city we know today, the legend speaks of Elissa, whom the poets called Dido. She fled from Tyre, on the other side of the sea, because her home was no longer safe. She reached the African coast with loyal people and little land. A local chief, smiling, said he would give her only the ground that fitted inside an oxhide. It looked like a small favour. Dido thanked him without arguing and took the hide home, already thinking calmly.",
    "The next night she cut the hide into very thin strips. With them, she tied a wide circle around a hill beside the sea. The chief had to keep his word. Stretched into threads, the hide covered far more than he had imagined. That is how Carthage was born, not from an army, but from a fair trick. The child of Tunis still hears this part as the best geometry lesson in the Mediterranean. A long line, well used, encloses a city. Dido's cunning was the first throne.",
    "The years passed and the poets, above all Virgil, told another face of the same queen. Dido welcomed Aeneas, a traveller whom fate was calling far away. The two had a deep friendship by the sea. When his ship had to leave, the queen was left with an enormous sadness, the kind a whole city feels. The old verses speak of a farewell fire on the coast. From children, Tunisia does not ask for that fire. It asks that they remember a founder who also knew how to love and lose.",
    "So there are two Didos in the same story, and both are one person. The first is the woman who turns an oxhide into a map. The second is the queen whose farewell stayed in the poems. Yasmine, listening to her grandmother in the medina, understood that she did not have to choose. The Punic coast is still under the modern city, in the stones and in the names. The hill surrounded by strips still teaches that cleverness fits in a small gesture. A kingdom can begin without shouting.",
    "Tunisia keeps Dido as a founder, with the trick, the hide and the throne. If you ever go to Carthage, look at the sea and imagine the strips of leather drawing the first street. The legend asks for respect for both memories. One is the cleverness that builds. The other is the longing the poets did not want to erase. It is not a war story. It is the story of a woman. With one hide and many loyal people, she gave a home to a port. The Mediterranean still measures cities by that line.",
  ],
  "tn:1": [
    "Between Tozeur and Douz, in southern Tunisia, the Sahara does not hide chests at first sight. It hides wells, palms and old rules. Caravans crossed this sand in Roman times and long after. The tales speak of jinns who guard the oases, not as monsters, but as doorkeepers. One of them left a simple law. The well opens for whoever gives water to the camel first, and not to himself. Yasmine heard this from her grandfather before a short trip between dunes, with a water bottle and a patient animal.",
    "The sun was strong, her tongue was dry and the camel walked with its head down. Yasmine saw a glint among the rocks and ran, already opening the water bottle for herself. She remembered her grandfather's sentence in time and stopped. She poured the first water into the camel's bowl, and it drank slowly. Only then did she wet her own mouth. The sand by the rocks gave way a little. A narrow well appeared, with dark, cool water. Nobody clapped. The desert teaches this exchange every day, with no audience.",
    "Further on, a hurried traveller was digging in the dune. He had dreamt of a chest of gold. His camel waited, forgotten, and the man's water bottle was turned only towards his own thirst. As soon as the spade hit an old wooden frame, the wind got up. The mouth of the nearby well filled with sand. A calm voice, with no face, said: ‘The treasure is not the chest.’ The traveller went pale. Yasmine recognised the doorkeeper of the oasis, the jinn of the story. He was blocking the water of a greedy digger.",
    "She offered the traveller what was left in her bottle, but only after his camel had drunk. She helped him fill in the hole of the false treasure and say sorry in a low voice. The sand left the well the way it had come in, with no crash. The palms, the dates and the shade remained, and they were the real gold. The man put away his spade and went on more slowly. Yasmine understood that a jinn, in this story, does not come to scare children. It reminds us of the right order: first the one who carries you, then you.",
    "The Sahara of Tunisia does not need buried coins to be rich. The child, among palms, learns that the treasure is the shade, the date and the well. Without kindness to the camel, there is no journey. Without a journey, there is no tale. If you find water in the desert, do not open it only for yourself. Do not dig the ground looking for a coffer. Give water to those who walk with you. The invisible doorkeeper, whether a jinn or the law of the sand, leaves the door open for those who share.",
  ],
  "tn:2": [
    "In the medinas of Tunis and Sfax, Ali Baba is told under the arches of the souk. He is told as if it were real. The next street could be the street of the story. It is the same tale from the Thousand and One Nights. It came with Arab folklore and stayed here, because the Tunisia of little lanes is a natural stage. Ali Baba was poor and honest. One day, hidden behind baskets, he heard robbers say ‘Open Sesame’ in front of a door in the rock. The door opened. He waited for them to leave and went in. He took only a small sack and went home.",
    "His brother Cassim heard about the gold and wanted the whole cave. He shouted the word and filled so many sacks that he forgot the phrase to get out. The door shut with him inside, frightened in the dark. The tale the children hear has not a drop of blood in it. Ali Baba came back, said ‘Open Sesame’ and brought his brother out, pale and ashamed. The word had opened. Greed had closed. Cassim promised not to go back alone. For a few days he even kept it, looking at the sack like a warning.",
    "The robbers found the house and pretended to be oil merchants. They left forty jars in the courtyard. Almost every one carried a quiet man inside, waiting for night. Only one held real oil. Morgiana, the servant, was watchful. She heard a muffled sneeze and saw a jar move. She did not cry out into the alley. She went to Ali Baba and said: ‘The house is not alone.’ Together they lit lanterns. Then they called the neighbours of the souk. Many eyes are worth more than one fight. The robbers fled through the lanes, empty-handed.",
    "The gold that remained was little. It was enough to pay debts, to buy bread and to give Morgiana her share, which nobody forgot. Cassim, this time, did not go back to the door in the rock. He had learned that a room full of coins, without the right word and without measure, is a trap. Ali Baba kept the magic phrase only to whisper it to children, like someone keeping a key. In the souk, someone still jokes and says ‘Open Sesame’ in front of a closed shop. Everyone laughs, because they know the ending.",
    "The real hero is not the coffer. It is Morgiana, who saw, thought, and saved the house without turning the courtyard into a scene of fear. The story does not change from Tunis to Sfax. The word opens, greed closes, the servant saves. The gold of the forty jars still fits in a lane, if imagination is the measure. Yasmine, passing under the arches, repeats the phrase and remembers to tell Morgiana's name as well. Without that name, the souk would keep only the shine, and the shine was never the heart of the tale.",
  ],
  "tn:3": [
    "Djerba, in southern Tunisia, is an island of weavers, low houses, and guests who arrive and take their time leaving. Some link it to the lotus-eaters of Homer. These were the people in the poem who offered a sweet fruit, the lotus, and the traveller forgot his ship. Djerbans smile when they hear this and neither confirm nor deny it. They prefer another image. The island is an enormous turtle that one day stopped in the sea and stayed. Its shell is the land. The water around was the one the animal chose for resting.",
    "Yasmine went to spend a few days at the house of a weaver aunt. In the port, a traveller ate a sweet slice of fruit and sat down in the shade. He said he no longer knew the name of his own neighbourhood. He laughed, as if forgetting were a gift. Her aunt shook her head with humour, not with anger. ‘The turtle likes those who stay,’ she said. ‘But too sweet a lotus is a warning.’ The island's hospitality is real. The tea appears, the loom keeps company, and nobody pushes the guest to the boat before time.",
    "The traveller slept two nights. On the third he was already calling neighbours by name, without remembering the quay he had left from. The turtle of the legend, slow as the pace of Djerba, did not hold him with ropes. It held him with comfort. Yasmine took him to see the sea on the outside of the shell, so to speak, at the water's edge. She asked him to say the name of his street three times. On the third, memory came back a little, like a tide. He thanked her, embarrassed, and kept the sweet fruit for the end of the meal.",
    "Homer may have dreamt this island, or may have dreamt another. Djerba leaves the doubt in the air with a smile. What the aunt wanted to teach fits in one sentence. Whoever arrives may stay, and must still remember home. The turtle is not a prison. It is an ancient animal that stopped because it found the place good. The looms, the open doors and the tea are part of the shell. The lotus, if it covers the name of the street, stops being a treat and becomes fog. Sweetness needs a shore.",
    "Yasmine went back to the mainland with a woven cloth and the story of the turtle in her pocket. She tells it with the humour of the south. The island is a shell and the sea is the chosen water. Even so, a happy guest should still know where they live. Tunisia does not confirm Homer and has no need to. The slow step, the hospitality and the warning are enough. If one day someone offers you too sweet a fruit in Djerba, eat it with pleasure. Say the name of your home before the last bite.",
  ],
  "tn:4": [
    "Tozeur, in inland Tunisia, is an oasis where palms grow in rows, like verses. Between them are corridors of shade they call corbeilles. The air underneath is another country, cool, while outside the Sahara waits. Yasmine walked with her grandfather, eating a date. She asked where so much water came from. Her grandfather said the water there has a person's name. Without it, the sand swallows the gardens. With it, there are poems, dates and children running barefoot in the green dark.",
    "One legend tells of a saint who arrived with a staff and saw the people thirsty. He did not shout at the sand. He asked permission and put his staff to the ground. A spring rose, thin at first, then enough to fill the channels. Another legend tells of a woman who wept for her people. Her tears found a way and became a river. Her grandfather did not fight with the two versions. He said asking, weeping and digging are three ways of the same miracle. The oasis did not fall from the sky finished.",
    "The poets of Tozeur know every spring by name, as one knows aunts. One is named for saintly patience. Another is named for the woman of tears. Another is named for somebody's grandfather who dug up to his elbows. Yasmine repeated the names while the water ran quietly between the palms. She understood that drinking there was greeting people. The date in her mouth was sweet because someone, long ago, refused to let the desert have the last word. The waterworks, in this tale, are sacred and at the same time very practical.",
    "Her grandfather showed her a channel clogged with leaves and asked her to help clean it. There was no magic staff that afternoon, there were hands. When the water ran again, her grandfather spoke. ‘See, the saint began it, and we continue it.’ Yasmine laughed, her mouth still full of something sweet. She understood the whole lesson. An oasis dies if the grandchildren only eat the story and do not clear the channel. The legend does not replace the work. It gives the work a face, so that nobody treats water as an accident without an owner.",
    "At night the rows of palms looked like an open book. The desert stayed outside, huge and quiet. Tozeur shone within. The child who eats the date and hears the saint learns something. The water was asked for, wept for and dug for. That is why it has a person's name. Inland Tunisia teaches this without haste, in the shade. If you go to the oasis, drink slowly and ask what the spring is called. The answer is always a story, and the story is always someone who did not let thirst win.",
  ],
  "dz:0": [
    "Algiers comes down to the sea in white, tightly packed houses, with stairs that change their mind halfway. This maze is called the Casbah, and in legends it is not just a district. It is a character. It is said that a princess appeared on a balcony, facing the port. She kept a treasure hidden in the lanes. It was safe from the corsairs who wanted to take what was not theirs. She did not live in a European fairy-tale palace. Her kingdom was the lime wash, the blue of the doors and the smell of the sea below.",
    "One day Amel climbed up while playing and lost her aunt at a corner of steps. Every courtyard looked like the one before. A rose fell from a balcony and stopped at her feet, fresh, with no note. Amel looked up and thought she saw a girl in white smiling. But the sun was hitting the lime wash, and the figure vanished. A neighbour, at her door, spoke. ‘If the rose fell for you, it is because you did not come to take anything. The princess of the Casbah chooses those who smile.’ Amel held the flower in both hands.",
    "The neighbour told her that each age left a girl watching the port. The Ottomans, the French, the hard years, and then independence in 1962. That year Algeria dressed the princess in the clothes of the people. The treasure was not a coffer. It was the city refusing to be erased. The corsairs of the legend, and the powers that came later, always found a balcony. There someone watched the sea and said: this house is not empty. The rose was the short message of that long sentence.",
    "Amel went down slowly and found her aunt in the square, already worried. She showed the rose and told her about the balcony. Her aunt did not laugh at her. She said many children get lost in the Casbah. They come back sure they saw the princess, and that too is a way for the city to introduce itself. There is no need for a king's map. It is enough not to treat the stairs as a mute place. Amel put the rose in a glass at home. Each time she looked at it, she remembered to smile first.",
    "Urban Algeria tells the Casbah as one tells about a grandmother. It is told with pride, with curves and with secrets that are not for sale. The princess is still on the balcony whenever a child gets lost and is welcomed kindly. The white, the sea and the rose are enough to make a kingdom. If a flower falls in your path, do not take it as a prize in a race. Take it as a message that the city still chooses those who smile. Answer with the care you would use entering someone else's house.",
  ],
  "dz:1": [
    "In south-east Algeria, the Tassili n'Ajjer is a plateau of stone in the middle of the Sahara. By day it looks like nothing but desert. On the rocks, though, there are very old paintings. They show giraffes, cattle, people and figures with horns, drawn when this sand was still green. The Tuareg say the ancestors painted so the rain would remember the way back. The treasure here does not fit in a pocket. Amel went with a guide to see the panels. She kept her hands in her pockets and touched nothing.",
    "In front of an ochre giraffe, Amel stayed silent for quite a while. The guide said: ‘Where you see sand, they saw grass and water. The desert is an archive.’ UNESCO helps guard the stones. The Tuareg guard the stories that walk around them. The two guards are not enemies. Legend and science join hands. The climate changed, the rivers dried up, and the memory stayed on the wall. Amel imagined the ancient meadow. A line on the rock can be the last sentence of a river. Erasing it would be like closing a book.",
    "Further on, a visitor wanted to break off a chip, ‘just as a souvenir’. The guide stood in front of the wall, without shouting. He said that whoever breaks a line erases an ancient river. No copy in a pocket can replace the original. The man was ashamed, put away his pocket knife, and apologised to the stone. That made even the guide smile. Amel took a photograph with her mind, not with a need to carry it away. The treasure of the Tassili is looked at. If you take it, it becomes a wound.",
    "They sat in the shade of a rock arch. The guide told them that, on the routes, people still speak of the rain as of a relative who travelled. The paintings are the message left on the door: come back, you know the way. Nobody guarantees that the rain reads ochre. The legend is not a weather report. It is a way of treating memory with respect. Amel drank from her bottle slowly. She thought of the giraffes that no longer walk there, and of the painted cattle that prove a different Sahara.",
    "Back in the city, Amel showed drawings she had made, humble copies, and said the original stays on the stone. The Algerian child, looking at photographs of the paintings, hears that the desert was once a meadow. That sentence changes the whole map. The treasure of the Tassili is not sold. It is watched, told and protected. If you ever enter a painted cave, leave the line where the ancestors put it. The rain, if it wants to remember, needs the whole sentence, and so do you.",
  ],
  "dz:2": [
    "In southern Algeria, the Tuareg say the camel reads the sky better than a hurried traveller. On a windless night, a caravan lost its track between dunes that looked the same. The people argued, each pointing a different way. Thirst had already made the voices loud. There was a child, Amel, and an old camel that took no part in the argument. The animal looked at a low, bright star and began to walk. The guide said: ‘We follow it. It does not give up when we want to turn back out of stubbornness.’",
    "They walked for hours. The sand changed its name on every slope. The Tuareg say the camel knows twenty of those names, while a hurried person sees only one: sand. Amel held on to the rope and kept repeating the star, so as not to lose sight of it. There was no map. There was the animal's step and a point of light. Someone suggested stopping to wait for day. The camel refused to lie down, as if the well had a fixed time. The star looked like a patient lantern at the edge of the sky.",
    "Just before dawn, the ground turned darker and damp. A stone well appeared, half covered but alive. The camel drank first, as the desert demands, and only then did the caravan fill the water bottles. Amel pressed her forehead to the animal's warm neck and thanked it. In the sky, the same star seemed a little higher, already on its way to its place. The guide told the rest of the legend. The star, grateful to the beast that did not give up, stayed for ever pointing north. That way other caravans would not argue until hoarse.",
    "It is not a lesson meant to replace a metal compass, and the guide laughed at that. It is a tale that puts the needle in the right place. It belongs on the animal that knows the sand. It also belongs on the sky, which does not lie if you know how to look. Without the camel and without the star, crossing the Sahara blind is a dangerous folly. With both, the desert stops being only an emptiness and becomes a country with paths. Amel kept the picture even in the city: a rope, a star, a well at dawn.",
    "The Tuareg repeat this story to children so that hurry does not take charge of the route. The legend is a compass. The camel is the needle. If you get lost, do not shout in every direction at the same time. Choose whoever knows the ground. Follow the light that does not run away. Give water first to whoever carried you. Southern Algeria fits into this simple pair. The north of the sky, they say, still owes a favour to that stubborn camel. He would not lie down before the well.",
  ],
  "dz:3": [
    "In the oases of Algeria, folklore speaks of a jinn who lives in the well and near the bread oven. It is not a monster from a horror story. In the gentle version the grandparents prefer, it is an invisible neighbour, with rules. Jinns, in popular speech, are creatures of fire without smoke, and not all of them are bad. This one asks for salt and respect. If a traveller arrives giving orders, the water vanishes. If a traveller arrives asking, the palm gives one more patch of shade. Amel learned this before fetching water alone.",
    "That day, a passing man banged on the bucket and shouted at the well. ‘Come on, I give the orders here, bring water now.’ The bucket came up with dry sand. The palms seemed narrower, and the oven beside them left the bread pale. The man got angry, which did not help. Amel's grandmother did not speak of dreadful punishments. She said: ‘The oasis is small and cannot bear arrogance. The neighbour of the well closed the tap. He was shown no respect.’ Then she taught her granddaughter a good word for the dark.",
    "The word was bismillah, said in a voice that asks. Amel carried a pinch of salt in her palm, the way you carry a hello to a neighbour. She stopped at the edge of the well and said the word calmly. She laid the salt on a smooth stone. She asked for water for the house and for the traveller, who waited awkwardly in the shade. The bucket went down and came back full, cool, smelling of wet stone. Nobody saw a face at the bottom. The palm's shade seemed wide enough for the two of them to sit. The bread finished browning.",
    "The traveller drank, thanked her and, in turn, left a little salt. He did not shout again. The grandmother explained that the legend is not there to make people afraid at night. It works as manners. In a place where water is scarce, giving orders is a quick way to end up with nothing. Asking is an old way to fit everyone in. The jinn, in this tale, is the rulebook of the well: invisible, but clear. Amel laughed at a neighbour made of rules and fire without smoke, and still carried the salt the next day.",
    "In the towns and on the coast, many people in Algeria still say a kind word. They do it when they enter a dark place. They do not turn it into a fright. The oasis goes on teaching the same thing with palms. If you give orders to the water, it owes you no obedience. If you bring salt, respect and a request, there is shade enough. There is no need to see the neighbour. It is enough to treat the well as someone's home. The gentle version stays like this: good manners first, and the bucket comes up full.",
  ],
  "dz:4": [
    "On Fridays, in many Algerian homes, steam rises from a pot and couscous takes over the table. Some eat it with vegetables, or with tfaya, a sweet mix of onion and raisins. Others eat it with meat, depending on the house. Amel, watching her mother roll the semolina between wet hands, asked who had invented that. Her mother laughed and said Morocco, Tunisia and Algeria all like to be first. The legend, wiser than the argument, does not choose a flag. It chooses a woman with hands covered in flour.",
    "In olden times, a camp had very little grain and many hungry people. A woman refused to keep the handful for her own mat only. She wet her hands and rolled the semolina until the grains were loose and light. Then she cooked them in steam, over a vegetable broth. The little grew in volume and in smell. Some said a saint had guided her fingers. Some said hunger, well handled, teaches measure. The whole camp ate, and nobody was left off the plate. The miracle was not multiplying by noisy magic. It was not wasting.",
    "Since then, the gesture has been repeated until it became a rite. The hands that roll the semolina are the origin the legend accepts, whichever Maghreb country the pot is steaming in. Amel's mother put a grain on her daughter's tongue so she could feel the texture before the sauce. ‘A little, well treated, is enough,’ she said. Amel helped loosen the lumps with her fingertips, slowly, so as not to make mush. She understood that haste spoils couscous as greed spoils a well. Flour forgives you if you treat it with time.",
    "When the family sat down, there was room for a plate for the neighbour who knocked at the door. The story obliges it. There was no speech. There was steam, lemon and conversation. Amel thought of the woman of the camp and of the saint. Both of them could be her mother on any Friday. The politics of the house, in this tale, are simple. Food that reaches everyone makes a table bigger than an angry kingdom. Without the hands that roll, there is only a closed sack of grain.",
    "The Algerian child, on Friday, eats the origin without needing a manual. Couscous is rural, ancient and weekly. The legend of the flour that reaches everyone is still a lesson in measure. If one day you argue about which country the dish came from, smile and point to the hands. They are what made the little reach. The steam rises the same in Algiers, in Tunis or across the border. The miracle, modest and warm, is this. Treat well what seems not enough, until it is enough for one more person at the door.",
  ],
  "cm:0": [
    "In the wet forest of Cameroon, the spotted leopard boasted that he was the fastest of all. A snail, small and unhurried, heard him by a root. ‘Then let's race to the big tree,’ he said. The birds laughed. The leopard laughed more. The Bassa and Beti tales know this impossible race. It is sister to the tortoise and the hedgehog in other lands. Estelle, listening to her grandmother, was already cheering for the slow one. Her grandmother asked her to wait for the trick. In that forest, victory does not fit in paws alone.",
    "At night, the snail spoke with his cousins. There were many of them, the same size, with the same dark shell. Each went to wait quietly at a stretch of the path. One waited by the stream, one by the fallen trunk, one by the finish line. It was not magic. It was family. In the morning, the leopard scratched the ground and set off like a streak. As soon as he rounded the first bend, he heard a thin voice. ‘I'm already here.’ Further on, another identical shell repeated the sentence. He saw only a snail that seemed not to tire.",
    "The leopard ran until his breath hurt. The forest mud made him slip, and he got up dizzy. He was not really hurt, only ashamed and angrier and angrier. At every tree post, the same calm sentence. When at last he reached the finish line, panting, the snail of the line was sitting on a leaf. It looked washed and peaceful, as if it had spent the day at home. ‘I arrived first,’ he said. The cousins then came out of their places and greeted one another. The leopard understood the number and sat down. The wet forest laughed again.",
    "Nobody took away his strength or the beauty of his spots. They took away only the idea that speed alone always wins. The snail explained the game without cruelty. ‘You ran against a family, not against one foot.’ The leopard, after drinking at the stream, ended up laughing too. The lesson was old, and he had fallen into it with all four paws. Estelle, on the mat, imitated the snorting and then the slow step. Her grandmother said both imitations belong to the tale. Laughing at the strong only works if you see why the slow one arrived.",
    "Cameroon, which they call Africa in miniature, tells this story in many languages, and the lesson does not wear out. Family along the way is worth more than one creature's hurry. A snail with relatives is a slow nation, hard to beat. If you are fast like the leopard, do not despise those who seem still. There may be more people on that side than you think. If you are slow, do not go alone. Call your own. The equatorial forest keeps the message in the mud, beside the spotted footprints.",
  ],
  "cm:1": [
    "By the sea, in south-west Cameroon, there rises a mountain so big that the clouds get caught on its top. It is a volcano, still able to wake, and the villages on the slope know its smell. In Buea, Estelle saw the thin smoke and asked whether someone was cooking in the sky. Her grandmother said yes, in a way. The mountain is also called Mongo ma Ndemi, and the legend gives it the ways of a person. It is not a scene to photograph and forget. It is a tall neighbour, who sometimes stirs the pot.",
    "One story begins with a long quarrel among the hills. A tired giant lay down facing the sea and lit his pipe. His body became the mountain, the smoke became cloud, and he stayed resting. Another story says he is the cook of a hungry land, preparing a slow fire under the ground. Her grandmother liked both, because both ask for the same thing: attention. When the smell changes, the village reads the message, as one reads the sky before rain. The myth here is also a calm warning.",
    "Around the mountain, the earth is dark and fertile. Bananas grow, gardens grow, and trees that like old ash grow. Today and care go together. The same neighbour who offers good ground asks that nobody plays with the mouth of the fire. Estelle learned not to invent scary details. It was enough to say the giant sometimes turns over in bed. On those days people go a little lower down, calmly, and wait. There is no shouting in a well-told tale. There is respect for a fire nobody can see whole.",
    "One afternoon the summit changed colour and her grandmother covered the pot earlier. ‘Today the neighbour is cooking high,’ she said. They watched from far off, holding hands, until the smoke went back to its usual white thread. Nothing bad happened in their street. Even so, Estelle understood the report. Watching the mountain is part of living there, as watching the river is part of living by the water. Mongo ma Ndemi has a name, so it is not treated like any old hill. Someone with a name can be greeted, and taken seriously.",
    "The children of Buea grow up with this cloud on the horizon and bananas on the plate. The origin of the mountain, sleeping giant or cook of hunger, explains the smoke without taking away its mystery. South-west Cameroon did not separate the danger from the present. It gave them the same address. If you see the summit changing, do not go closer to prove your courage. Read the cloud, listen to those who live on the slope, and let the giant finish his fire. The mountain is not a postcard background. It is someone who still breathes.",
  ],
};
